#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c5560ec9-32d6-514d-92fa-fbd77500d943" :question/math-academy-id "e-4431" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/fc/fcc4c2fb5ffaf79bd40a967fa0f3ff3ed3c25a4bc91e831f679aa2162d93050b.svg) find ![](images/3a/3a950a6005df11dc3df83b40a796c8e5ce962b591e040207bb961da532ec92dd.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given two ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrices ![](images/34/34058a2a5d1de088feed7d9825ba218394c9a8bb880b046be7b47307cdd37ce1.svg) and ![](images/50/504b9ae6bd4255a78e26a2d32aaca2d613171ad01c9ac5d992f5512d093605e7.svg) we have\n\n![](images/3f/3fbf04c7a536c85422f2762eb5475ff5edfcff7a71eee0fa3cc14143e79bb10f.svg)\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n![](images/16/16c31a7e010da9d8f4e17628bc1c7b5dd3ef1d65a4e5e191bec2fd404135a7bc.svg)\n\nWe can compute the required determinants as follows:\n\n- For the first determinant, we notice that it's upper triangular. Therefore, the determinant is simply the product of the diagonal entries.\n\n- For the second determinant, we can compute it using a Laplace expansion down the ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)st column.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n![](images/27/273e8879a6f78121b98902852e974aef15d9408e7cd026d3cc17e51837248ede.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "17487f8f-4974-5aaf-8729-6b1336ca5eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-4432" :question/problem "Find $|A^{-1}|,$ if  ![](images/8e/8e92cd7ddbecf627452e4fa63d2af25fc60fad228bf42b5c4f8c9dfad28f4971.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|A^{-1}| = \\dfrac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 14 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-12+7) \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\det(A^{-1}) =  \\dfrac{1}{|A|} = -\\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](images/58/589324d1b345360020e9c2b1096b64a08008a52b05a13fdc7fd39bcb063a8d58.svg)\nfind \n![](images/f4/f4925a93ca952863cac520a853c1bc5d7c815dc8324043d6c0963d20d2b2b1ff.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b01122-cef4-57e0-bbe6-9e9ca46a105d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263225} {:answer/id #uuid "68462165-dd02-5aef-a990-fa2a80304b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263899} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4b1f65-f423-5f95-a77f-398f082a7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264347} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c540ce-78c8-5ba3-8e16-45516155084a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264543} {:answer/id #uuid "da01d959-45b6-560c-907e-0539a9249ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4b1f65-f423-5f95-a77f-398f082a7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264347} :answer-field/id #uuid "b70c3988-227e-5d92-a2bc-8cd2a832cea5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aff6f4ac-0990-556a-848c-2e5cb8fef1cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130634" :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{aligned}|=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 78\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160+0 & 184+0 & 76+2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160+0 & 184+0 & 76+2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 76\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 160 & 184 & 76\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 & 76 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0+2(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}160 & 184 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (160\\cdot 4-184\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =176\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fcef5e-b3e2-5ccd-ad74-3f0f2bf5215e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327159} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef0a0f8-a3e1-5867-a44d-53d8501860b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328178} {:answer/id #uuid "9b76b2b0-7b79-5bed-b323-bb17d564fb79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328279} {:answer/id #uuid "c5b548cb-6708-57bd-8aac-098ad056ea48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328668} {:answer/id #uuid "e9942f7d-5789-519b-8b26-e8f47afefdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19fcef5e-b3e2-5ccd-ad74-3f0f2bf5215e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327159} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1c7dcea-54be-5b44-9e93-100efe093b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b66a6d15-0b61-5b61-a152-b57545d60ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42581" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|=-7$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+3)-1 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-7}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-7+(-6+20) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} {:answer/id #uuid "104af8d6-6df5-59fb-8200-c18490b4fd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327079} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8e5b8a-f047-5394-af88-102417a6f0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327338} {:answer/id #uuid "3fde6c7e-e80c-5ec0-964a-81bf5acc2144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327492} {:answer/id #uuid "c150ce04-23ed-5152-a4c5-802328f796f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b298ccb-6d42-51e0-bec7-bdcce97b859a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327030} :answer-field/id #uuid "7775f4a0-2beb-5ee4-8f08-e48b58e45535" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e713fddd-f608-51f9-ac5a-f16a9c918c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-42584" :question/problem "Find $|{A}^{-1}|,$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $|A|$ using a Laplace expansion across the $2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(12-12)+(-15+24)+2(10-16) \\\\  & =0+9-12 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n|{A}^{-1}|=\\frac{1}{|A|}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "884c1925-0ccc-54e5-8235-15f4852ab5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} {:answer/id #uuid "974ae98b-f65f-5d56-abb2-235fcf5d1709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118810} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f4f7fc-49f1-51bb-a52f-e78439376e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118811} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c1329a-446a-56c8-94dc-f3d8fb69e271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118812} {:answer/id #uuid "665f03b3-8fe1-5194-816a-e67e96a71c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "884c1925-0ccc-54e5-8235-15f4852ab5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118809} :answer-field/id #uuid "56464aea-61e2-5f04-a701-b6c9323c4b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daa9792e-2290-5f44-bdfd-5cde9ef8123b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42996" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa174c3-be74-5a68-a41e-293625f948d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327165} {:answer/id #uuid "45a4f0b7-ab1c-507f-a3c0-51640e4f16be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327543} {:answer/id #uuid "5200235b-4b2e-514a-8db5-4aa6adadf377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327648} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f8baab-a97f-50d5-965a-f8b1d544e5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328357} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8e009-9985-5a9f-88ca-075fd371139e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8e009-9985-5a9f-88ca-075fd371139e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329000} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c99cfa-c616-5ee5-9ec1-28b017f9b14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f613c1e6-df74-5921-9adf-aac6f2ef23de" :question/math-academy-id "q-43063" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12+3 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dafe486-15fc-5abc-8a0d-2beb22386f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420809} {:answer/id #uuid "8e814c6a-3786-561d-9f46-38ef12a0585a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420818} {:answer/id #uuid "90c1ce8c-7b18-5377-9a47-ace432f229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420819} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7cb828-c853-54d8-b205-d802c97b37f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420830} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b62576-113c-5f4d-b29d-3ce30a3f8585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90c1ce8c-7b18-5377-9a47-ace432f229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420819} :answer-field/id #uuid "660640e3-e8ef-509a-a758-3c65a1428537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37f105cd-9dd0-500b-bb29-7f9f80a1a14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43071" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "164c56ea-afbb-5ce4-800a-19002695db76" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of the Inverse of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b4b8b-cc0b-53a3-8c11-0c11e0db776d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327223} {:answer/id #uuid "238bb388-343a-58d7-9db2-050e57ec45b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327242} {:answer/id #uuid "68c0d0ce-8c27-5888-ace5-002687a4df64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327837} {:answer/id #uuid "c160f3eb-2949-5657-b5a9-161a21d65fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328622} {:answer/id #uuid "ce09f426-6762-5fb0-b6f0-768bffc90ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c160f3eb-2949-5657-b5a9-161a21d65fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328622} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87a46a6-b339-5dc3-8ffd-5e9183021584" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d375fe07-2a11-5b83-b2d9-76a00ed1fc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-43096" :question/problem "Find $det({A}^{-1}),$ if $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given an invertible $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $det(A):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-12-9 \\\\  & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\ndet({A}^{-1})=\\frac{1}{det(A)}=-\\frac{1}{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f2260d-7908-5a17-9d38-a9d0f295722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "754" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351894} {:answer/id #uuid "282dddef-1dc6-5f06-bffc-e9ed4063e18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351938} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8c6043-56a0-5050-9049-631d3e6340c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352116} {:answer/id #uuid "67e126ef-c44f-577d-99ef-b5c018ad680d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352493} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece99f2-c460-5d4a-a2ad-898a007dd715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b8c6043-56a0-5050-9049-631d3e6340c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352116} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4540e0b-356d-5436-a082-05ee75890a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af3f4732-8a8f-51ae-b35b-754f1abeb181" :question/math-academy-id "q-88266" :question/problem "Given that $B=[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(B).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(24-(-5))\\cdot (-14-(-12)) \\\\  & =29\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-58.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420849 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a67a7f-7ce3-5fc4-9a99-c7b43778cf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420810} {:answer/id #uuid "755ff62c-e4b0-56e4-8933-fdd296936d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420815} {:answer/id #uuid "9577d942-a37d-5bb6-ad63-4cff94938a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420820} {:answer/id #uuid "b74bd5d3-d00b-534f-908c-090b06c3201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420823} {:answer/id #uuid "c498389f-d133-538a-bef9-88e693ebc92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "755ff62c-e4b0-56e4-8933-fdd296936d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420815} :answer-field/id #uuid "633fd2f4-984e-51bc-b51a-5f37a89e06b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38d108f6-377b-5951-bc6b-4ec43d8b652b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88281" :question/problem "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],$ find $det(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that both matrices are triangular. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2217be1b-0e43-59a5-97bb-6d402bea3ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351875} {:answer/id #uuid "74fa8be3-ab54-58c6-8925-9df3ccda76de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352594} {:answer/id #uuid "93ed11fc-9c76-519e-b094-03edef2b5efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352848} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f75795-6763-534e-888d-3664209ea4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "202" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352959} {:answer/id #uuid "c12ae922-eac4-5656-80c2-43cb7caa50fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ed11fc-9c76-519e-b094-03edef2b5efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352848} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a4bf2b-6495-570d-a5cc-83942fc77250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da76c31c-efa0-5055-96f9-ec1ecc76273f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88304" :question/problem "Find\n$|\\begin{aligned}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
